Because the id is null until the entity is persisted. Add a transient entity to a `HashSet`, save it, and its hash code changes — the object is now in the wrong bucket and `contains()` returns false for an object that is demonstrably in the set. Bidirectional `@OneToMany` collections mapped as `Set` hit this constantly.

There are two defensible approaches.

**Use a business key** — something naturally unique and immutable: an order number, an email, an ISBN. This is the correct answer when one exists.

**When there is no business key**, use the shape Hibernate's own documentation recommends: a constant hash code, and equality on id with a null guard.

```java
@Override
public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) return true;
    if (!(o instanceof Order other)) return false;
    return id != null && id.equals(other.id);   // unsaved entities are equal only to themselves
}

@Override
public int hashCode() { return getClass().hashCode(); }   // constant: stable across the id being assigned
```

A constant hash code looks wrong until you see why: it degrades a `HashSet` of entities to a linear scan, which is fine because those sets are small, and it is the only value that does not change when the id is assigned. Note also `instanceof` rather than `getClass() ==` — Hibernate proxies are subclasses, so a `getClass()` comparison fails against a lazy proxy of the same row.
